===== REPLY =====

Read lib/csv_parser.py. parse_line() already handles quoting; the gap is
tests, not behavior, so this only adds tests/test_csv_parser.py.

Three cases, one test each:

    def test_quoted_field_with_embedded_comma():
        assert parse_line('a,"b,c",d') == ["a", "b,c", "d"]

    def test_empty_field_between_commas():
        assert parse_line("a,,c") == ["a", "", "c"]

    def test_file_with_no_trailing_newline(tmp_path):
        p = tmp_path / "rows.csv"
        p.write_bytes(b"a,b,c")  # no trailing \n
        assert list(parse_file(str(p))) == [["a", "b", "c"]]

The third one is the one worth flagging: parse_file() reads with
splitlines(), which already tolerates a missing trailing newline, so this
test passed on the first run rather than catching anything. Leaving it in
anyway — it is exactly the kind of edge a future change could break
silently.

